===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|Israel}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|Israel}}, from {{der|en|grc|Ἰσραήλ}}, from {{der|en|he|יִשְׂרָאֵל||Israel|tr=yisra'él}}.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref> In the Bible, it is said to be from the name {{m|he|[[שרה|יִשְׂרֶה]] [[אֵל]]|tr=yisré 'él}} given to [[Jacob]] in {{R:Tanach|1|32|29}}, after which his descendants came to be known as [[Israelite]]s and their land as the "kingdom/land of Israel". The personal name, already attested in [[Eblaite]] {{l|xeb|𒅖𒊏𒅋}} (''iš-ra-il'') and [[Ugaritic]] {{l|uga|𐎊𐎌𐎗𐎛𐎍}}<!-- as a personal name-->,<ref>Michael G. Hasel, ''Domination and Resistance: Egyptian Military Activity in the Southern Levant'' (Brill, 1998)</ref> has been variously translated as "he wrestles with God", "he prevails with God",<ref>James P. Boyd, ''Bible dictionary'' (Ottenheimer Publishers, 1958)</ref> or "God rules" (possibly from the same root {{m|he|ש־ר־ה}}/{{m|he|ש־ר־ר}} as {{m|he|שָׂרָה||Sarah}}).<ref>Victor P. Hamilton, ''The Book of Genesis: Chapters 18–50'' (NICOT, Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1995)</ref>

[[:File:YsyriAr.gif|Line 27]] of the [[w:Merneptah Stele|Merneptah Stele]] (from about 1200 {{B.C.E.}}) is thought to contain the earliest attestation of the name ''Israel'' in any language, {{cog|egy|ysrjꜣr}}.
